<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Olbia Pontica</h1><p class="st-meta">550 BCE – 375 CE (approx.) · capital Olbia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Greek city on the northern Black Sea coast, a trading gateway between the Mediterranean world and the Scythian steppe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a>, meaning 'prosperous', around 550 BCE on the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a>, near an older Greek trading post on Berezan island. The site, close to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, gave access to the grain lands and river routes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scythia">Scythia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a> was a democratic polis with an assembly, council and annual magistrates, and it cast distinctive bronze coins shaped like dolphins. Its merchants exported grain, fish and slaves and imported wine, oil and luxury goods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> visited the city, and his story of the Scythian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scyles">Scyles</a>, who secretly lived as a Greek there, shows how closely the two worlds mixed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a> resisted a siege by Alexander's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zopyrion">Zopyrion</a> in 331 BCE but was badly sacked by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> around 55 BCE. Rebuilt on a smaller scale, it came under Roman protection in the 2nd century CE. Gothic attacks in the 3rd century and the Hunnic invasion in the 4th brought urban life to an end.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 550 BCE – 375 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:48.76%;width:18.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 BCE – 375 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Olbia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Olbia, Borysthenes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-88&amp;focus=olbia-pontica" aria-label="Olbia Pontica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5430 537 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Olbia Pontica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5750 793L5813 846M5750 793L5552 825M5750 793L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5813 846h0M5552 825h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5750 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5750 793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Olbia. <a href="/atlas?year=-88&amp;focus=olbia-pontica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

